{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ShpRbaHqtcXDBxUyEEKJr7","type":"tool_result","content":"95\t### Flow 5 — `idempotency.test.ts`\n96\t\n97\t| TC | Test | Setup | Key assertions |\n98\t|----|------|-------|----------------|\n99\t| TC-5.1 | full resend | record 5, resend same 5 | zero recorded; five skips with `duplicate_idempotency_key`; read-back unchanged |\n100\t| TC-5.2 | partial resend | 3 recorded + 2 new | three skips, two recorded; orders continue dense (no gaps from skips) |\n101\t| TC-5.3 | cross-thread keys | same key, two threads | both record |\n102\t| TC-5.4 | skips inert | resend recorded prompt + turn_end mid-conversation | turn count/states unchanged; no transitions in result; work-item count unchanged; no duplicate message |\n103\t| TC-5.5 | key wins | key K with payload A recorded; resend K with valid payload B | skipped; read-back returns A; B nowhere in any table (`openRaw` scan) |\n104\t\n105\t### CLI process suite — `cli-process.test.ts` (verify-all only)\n106\t\n107\tRepresentative boundary proofs, not full TC repetition: `new-thread` → `resolve` round-trip; `message-events` via real stdin (including the 300KB tool result); TTY/empty stdin → `empty_stdin`, exit 1; one validation failure → JSON error shape, exit 1; `--help` and unknown command exit codes.\n108\t\n109\t## Architecture-Risk Tests (non-TC)\n110\t\n111\tBehaviors no TC names but the design promises; each exists because AC/TC mapping alone would miss it.\n112\t\n113\t| Risk | Test | Why TCs miss it |\n114\t|------|------|-----------------|\n115\t| Atomicity under mid-walk failure | induced failure inside the transaction (test seam closes handle) → read-back equals baseline | TC-4.3 covers validation rejection; this covers in-transaction failure |\n116\t| Restart survival | record batch, close handle, reopen → identical read-back including work items | NFR, no TC; the durability claim itself |\n117\t| Rejected batch takes no lock | concurrent open succeeds while rejection-path runs | design promise (pure validation before transaction), invisible to single-connection TCs |\n118\t| system_error rollback parity | storage failure mid-batch → rollback whole, `system_error` class | AC-4.6 is class-independent; TCs only exercise caller-error rejection |\n119\t| Fixture validity | builder defaults decode against real schemas | test substrate correctness, not product behavior |\n120\t| Boundary-check self-test | sabotage import fails the script | guards the guard |\n121\t| Verification gates fail correctly | failing test fails `verify`; edited Red file fails `green-verify` | proves the gates once (Chunk 0), then trusted |\n122\t| Registry lazy-init non-creation | reads against absent registry create no file | easy to get accidentally wrong with open-or-create storage helpers |\n123\t\n124\t## Coverage Summary\n125\t\n126\t| Flow | TCs | Supplemental | Est. tests |\n127\t|------|-----|--------------|-----------|\n128\t| 1 | 6 | lazy-init | 9 |\n129\t| 2 | 9 | — | 12 |\n130\t| 3 | 8 | golden suite | 14 |\n131\t| 4 | 5 | strictness, empty-fields | 10 |\n132\t| 5 | 5 | — | 7 |\n133\t| CLI process | — | boundary proofs | 6 |\n134\t| Architecture-risk | — | 8 risks | 9 |\n135\t| Chunk 0 smoke | — | rail, fixtures, tokenizer, gates | 5 |\n136\t| **Total** | **33** | | **~72** |\n137\t\n138\tWithin the epic's 60–72 estimate, at the top because architecture-risk tests are enumerated rather than folded in. Every TC from the epic appears exactly once as an owned row; the two cross-chunk deferrals (TC-1.4 → Chunk 2; TC-3.3/3.6 work halves → Chunk 5) are marked at their rows and owed by their receiving chunks.\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d47137f-cd86-4c15-bbf8-4a8dc794929e","uuid":"afab5b83-5d2f-4f3e-b068-d25271daf8a0","timestamp":"2026-06-10T12:33:22.239Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md","content":"### Flow 5 — `idempotency.test.ts`\n\n| TC | Test | Setup | Key assertions |\n|----|------|-------|----------------|\n| TC-5.1 | full resend | record 5, resend same 5 | zero recorded; five skips with `duplicate_idempotency_key`; read-back unchanged |\n| TC-5.2 | partial resend | 3 recorded + 2 new | three skips, two recorded; orders continue dense (no gaps from skips) |\n| TC-5.3 | cross-thread keys | same key, two threads | both record |\n| TC-5.4 | skips inert | resend recorded prompt + turn_end mid-conversation | turn count/states unchanged; no transitions in result; work-item count unchanged; no duplicate message |\n| TC-5.5 | key wins | key K with payload A recorded; resend K with valid payload B | skipped; read-back returns A; B nowhere in any table (`openRaw` scan) |\n\n### CLI process suite — `cli-process.test.ts` (verify-all only)\n\nRepresentative boundary proofs, not full TC repetition: `new-thread` → `resolve` round-trip; `message-events` via real stdin (including the 300KB tool result); TTY/empty stdin → `empty_stdin`, exit 1; one validation failure → JSON error shape, exit 1; `--help` and unknown command exit codes.\n\n## Architecture-Risk Tests (non-TC)\n\nBehaviors no TC names but the design promises; each exists because AC/TC mapping alone would miss it.\n\n| Risk | Test | Why TCs miss it |\n|------|------|-----------------|\n| Atomicity under mid-walk failure | induced failure inside the transaction (test seam closes handle) → read-back equals baseline | TC-4.3 covers validation rejection; this covers in-transaction failure |\n| Restart survival | record batch, close handle, reopen → identical read-back including work items | NFR, no TC; the durability claim itself |\n| Rejected batch takes no lock | concurrent open succeeds while rejection-path runs | design promise (pure validation before transaction), invisible to single-connection TCs |\n| system_error rollback parity | storage failure mid-batch → rollback whole, `system_error` class | AC-4.6 is class-independent; TCs only exercise caller-error rejection |\n| Fixture validity | builder defaults decode against real schemas | test substrate correctness, not product behavior |\n| Boundary-check self-test | sabotage import fails the script | guards the guard |\n| Verification gates fail correctly | failing test fails `verify`; edited Red file fails `green-verify` | proves the gates once (Chunk 0), then trusted |\n| Registry lazy-init non-creation | reads against absent registry create no file | easy to get accidentally wrong with open-or-create storage helpers |\n\n## Coverage Summary\n\n| Flow | TCs | Supplemental | Est. tests |\n|------|-----|--------------|-----------|\n| 1 | 6 | lazy-init | 9 |\n| 2 | 9 | — | 12 |\n| 3 | 8 | golden suite | 14 |\n| 4 | 5 | strictness, empty-fields | 10 |\n| 5 | 5 | — | 7 |\n| CLI process | — | boundary proofs | 6 |\n| Architecture-risk | — | 8 risks | 9 |\n| Chunk 0 smoke | — | rail, fixtures, tokenizer, gates | 5 |\n| **Total** | **33** | | **~72** |\n\nWithin the epic's 60–72 estimate, at the top because architecture-risk tests are enumerated rather than folded in. Every TC from the epic appears exactly once as an owned row; the two cross-chunk deferrals (TC-1.4 → Chunk 2; TC-3.3/3.6 work halves → Chunk 5) are marked at their rows and owed by their receiving chunks.\n","numLines":45,"startLine":95,"totalLines":139}}}
